    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Another common standardization in the literature is Hedge's g, which uses $$ g_{i}=\\frac{\\overline{z}_{i,t}-\\overline{z}_{i,c}}{s_{i,p}},\\quad\\mathrm{w h e r e}\\quad s_{i,p}^{2}=\\frac{(n_{i,t}-1)s_{i,t}^{2}+(n_{i,c}-1)s_{i,c}^{2}}{n_{i,c}+n_{i,t}-2} $$ which is the unbiased OLS estimate (and hence the REML estimate) of the pooled standard error. One further correction is often applied to $ g_i $, in that the ratio given by Equation A4.28a is a biased estimator of $ (\\mu_t - \\mu_c)/\\sigma_p $, which can be adjusted by using $$ g_{i}\\left(1-\\frac{3}{4(n_{i,c}+n_{i,t})-9}\\right) $$",
      "source_page": 1252,
      "source_pages": [
        1252
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix4:p023:b12",
        "Evolution_appendix4:p023:b13",
        "Evolution_appendix4:p023:b15",
        "Evolution_appendix4:p023:b16"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
