    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Besides comparisons of means, a meta-analysis often examines studies measuring the association between two variables, in which case the Pearson correlation coefficient, $ r_{i} $, is the initial summary statistic. This is a slightly biased estimator of the true correlation coefficient ($ \\rho $), but a simple correction returns an unbiased estimate, $$ r_{i}^{*}=r_{i}+\\frac{r_{i}(1-r_{i}^{2})}{2(n_{i}-3)} $$",
      "source_page": 1252,
      "source_pages": [
        1252
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix4:p023:b18",
        "Evolution_appendix4:p023:b19"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          132.0,
          952.0,
          906.0,
          1046.0
        ],
        [
          428.0,
          1069.0,
          605.0,
          1120.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "80dc820ba497cc942916434c24b45b7ec99e53290cb54d2286ebc28ff0d32d3c"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Fisher's z-transformation is used to both stabilize the variance and remove some of the skew: $$ z_{i,r}=\\frac{1}{2}\\ln\\left(\\frac{1+r_{i}}{1-r_{i}}\\right),\\qquad\\mathrm{w i t h}\\qquad s_{i}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n_{i}-3} $$",
      "source_page": 1252,
      "source_pages": [
        1252
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix4:p023:b21",
        "Evolution_appendix4:p023:b22"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          131.0,
          1142.0,
          904.0,
          1188.0
        ],
        [
          313.0,
          1186.0,
          722.0,
          1236.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "c8ca3e9e299198c2be48b7b874e790a09207cf0c617cd07da5a8dad37545f867"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Typically the unbiased estimate $ (r^{*}) $ is used in place of r in the transformed data, and we use $ T_{i} = z_{i,r} $ as the study effect (i.e., the value in Table A4.3). Note that the standard error of the transformed sample correlation is now simply a function of the sample size. Likewise, any estimates based on z can be back-converted to inferences of $ \\rho $ by using Fisher's z-to-r transform $$ r=\\frac{e^{2z_{r}}-1}{e^{2z_{r}}+1} $$",
      "source_page": 1252,
      "source_pages": [
        1252
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix4:p023:b24",
        "Evolution_appendix4:p023:b25"
      ],
